WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.976
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4674.

2
00:00:00.976 --> 00:00:01.952
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:01.952 --> 00:00:04.554
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alrighty, I'm here today with Ms. Theresa Lee.

4
00:00:04.554 --> 00:00:06.181
It's Wednesday January the eleventh.

5
00:00:06.181 --> 00:00:07.807
My first interview of 2017.

6
00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:08.133
<v THERESA LEE>Really?

7
00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.434
<v ARSENEAULT>It's a special moment.

8
00:00:09.434 --> 00:00:16.590
And she's here to do a follow up interview to cover some topics that she didn't get to in her first interview.

9
00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:27.000
So Ms. Theresa, I think that you gave me a list here and I see the first thing we want to talk about is the Mossville Social and the Civic Club.

10
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:27.400
<v LEE>Right.

11
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.800
<v ARSENEAULT>Talk a little but about that.

12
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:36.200
<v LEE>Okay I did mention in the first interview that the club, the Social and Civic club,

13
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:43.400
was founded in February of 1975 and our two founders are Betty J. Williams and Joan Gilbert Rigmaiden.

14
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:51.000
And actually these two people are the only two in the club that are still living in Mossville [Louisiana].

15
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.000
The rest of us have moved away, but we still are members of that group.

16
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.800
<c.p>Through the years we sponsored several things.

17
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:06.600
When we really started in '75 we had the Santa's visit, Easter egg hunts for the children,

18
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.800
story hour, cartoon characters, we had teen dances, we even had Boozo Chavis.

19
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:17.400
He wasn't popular at the time, but we had him to play for us.

20
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.000
We sponsored sports teams.

21
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.000
We still visit nursing homes.

22
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.000
We purchased neighborhood watch signs.

23
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.400
We played a major role in getting the vote out for President Obama through phone banks,

24
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.800
buying and erecting yard signs, and even four large street signs.

25
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:39.400
So all persons who passed through Mossville knew that they were in Obama country.

26
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.800
We still visit churches leaving donations especially for the youth department.

27
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.000
We participate still in clothes, a clothes drive.

28
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:54.200
We contribute to persons with special needs and those persons who have suffered losses such as house fires.

29
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.000
We did that recently for Thanksgiving.

30
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.595
The Johnny Comeaux Award has now become the Richard Lee/Johnny Comeaux Award.

31
00:02:02.595 --> 00:02:09.122
G-Men Award, and it's given to athletes or artists who do well in school.

32
00:02:09.122 --> 00:02:14.250
We did this . . . These two people played at Grambling [University] together,

33
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:21.243
Johnny Comeaux in basketball and Richard Lee in football and both are natives of Mossville.

34
00:02:21.243 --> 00:02:31.500
The big thing we do every year, and we've done this since 1976 . . . now keep in mind we were founded in 1975,

35
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.628
but we began our Katherine Marie Jourdan Victorian Scholarship in April.

36
00:02:36.628 --> 00:02:41.757
I think it was April, maybe during the summer, of 1976.

37
00:02:41.757 --> 00:02:51.081
We did this because Katherine, we called her Myree, she was our first secretary and she died April of 1976.

38
00:02:51.081 --> 00:02:53.412
We still give this scholarship.

39
00:02:53.412 --> 00:03:01.338
We are a nonprofit organization and all our money comes from our pockets from dues and pledges.

40
00:03:01.338 --> 00:03:02.736
That's about it.

41
00:03:02.736 --> 00:03:05.068
We're still very . . . We're active.

42
00:03:05.068 --> 00:03:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Excellent.

43
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.745
<v LEE>And what we do in Mossville.

44
00:03:07.745 --> 00:03:08.909
<v ARSENEAULT>So Boo . . . I've heard . . .

45
00:03:08.909 --> 00:03:09.491
<v LEE>Boozo Chavis.

46
00:03:09.491 --> 00:03:12.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Could you tell me a little more about Boozo Chavis?

47
00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.055
<v LEE>Well all . . . I can't really remember a lot, but he was . . . The zydeco that's his thing.

48
00:03:17.055 --> 00:03:24.327
I'm trying to think how many years ago he has passed and they still carry on his tradition of having the parties in this area.

49
00:03:24.327 --> 00:03:26.655
But we had him at the teen dances.

50
00:03:26.655 --> 00:03:30.436
He played for us and some of the other dances that we had.

51
00:03:30.436 --> 00:03:35.382
That was way back in the '80s when he was still very young and could have these.

52
00:03:35.382 --> 00:03:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I know I've heard that name I just . . .

53
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:38.710
<v LEE>I know, yeah.

54
00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:39.419
<v ARSENEAULT>And it's B-U-Z-U?

55
00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:43.204
<v LEE>I think it's B-O-Z-A and then C-H-A-V-I-S [Lee notes correct name is: Wilson Anthony “Boozo” Chavis].

56
00:03:43.204 --> 00:03:45.570
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay I just wanted to make sure because I don't . . .

57
00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:46.753
<v LEE>I think that is correct.

58
00:03:46.753 --> 00:03:50.065
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . think we have it spelled right in our . . . When people mention it I don't . . .

59
00:03:50.065 --> 00:03:52.667
<v LEE>And I can always check and let you know about it.

60
00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:53.140
<v ARSENEAULT>Sure, sure.

61
00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:57.634
If I know . . . If it's B-U-Z-A I could probably look it up and his name will probably come up.

62
00:03:57.634 --> 00:03:57.871
<v LEE>Yeah.

63
00:03:57.871 --> 00:04:00.000
I don't know why I'm thinking it's B-O-O.

64
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:00.235
<v ARSENEAULT>B-O-O?

65
00:04:00.235 --> 00:04:00.471
<v LEE>O.

66
00:04:00.471 --> 00:04:00.706
<v ARSENEAULT>-Z-A?

67
00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:00.941
<v LEE>Yeah.

68
00:04:00.941 --> 00:04:01.647
<v ARSENEAULT>I got you.

69
00:04:01.647 --> 00:04:03.529
<v LEE>I think it's that, but I'm not sure.

70
00:04:03.529 --> 00:04:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

71
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.466
<v LEE>Last name was Chavis.

72
00:04:05.466 --> 00:04:05.832
C-H-A-V-I-S.

73
00:04:05.832 --> 00:04:06.931
<v ARSENEAULT>Good to know.

74
00:04:06.931 --> 00:04:12.061
<v LEE>There's something in 1983 I didn't mention earlier that we had our house fire.

75
00:04:12.061 --> 00:04:18.290
We had been living in the house for about ten years on Prater Road, 602 Prater Road.

76
00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:23.420
I tell you when things get tough you really know who your friends are.

77
00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:26.351
You really know how close a community is.

78
00:04:26.351 --> 00:04:33.679
One example of how close Mossville community was at that time is that love was just poured out to us.

79
00:04:33.679 --> 00:04:35.145
I mean poured out.

80
00:04:35.145 --> 00:04:44.305
People came and for instance she's not my sister-in-law anymore but still a very good friend and a member of Mossville Social and Civic Club,

81
00:04:44.305 --> 00:04:45.405
Barbara Sam Potier.

82
00:04:45.405 --> 00:04:49.802
She came and she brought me her leather jacket to put on.

83
00:04:49.802 --> 00:04:52.000
Things like that you know.

84
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.909
Richard's job, Conoco, supported us monetarily so [did] my job, Sulphur High School, Dawn's school.

85
00:04:57.909 --> 00:05:02.636
The teachers were very supportive in helping her through that emotional time.

86
00:05:02.636 --> 00:05:08.152
Harvey Istre, one of Richard's closet friends who also worked with him at Conoco,

87
00:05:08.152 --> 00:05:13.273
brought us a trailer to live in while our house was being rebuilt.

88
00:05:13.273 --> 00:05:14.455
Things like that.

89
00:05:14.455 --> 00:05:16.030
The late Reverend L.V.

90
00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:21.152
Young – he was the pastor of Morning Star Baptist Church in Vinton [Louisiana].

91
00:05:21.152 --> 00:05:22.727
They gave us money.

92
00:05:22.727 --> 00:05:32.970
Willow Springs Baptist Church, the pastor at that time was Harvey Gilliam, supported us and also sponsored, along with the family, a community shower for us.

93
00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:39.667
They did not leave out Dawn because she had her own special shower at her grandmother's house.

94
00:05:39.667 --> 00:05:41.636
So it was . . . it just

95
00:05:41.636 --> 00:05:42.424
<c.p>was awesome.

96
00:05:42.424 --> 00:05:44.000
It really was.

97
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.517
<v ARSENEAULT>The shower they just . . .?

98
00:05:45.517 --> 00:05:45.897
<v LEE>Whatever.

99
00:05:45.897 --> 00:05:50.828
House, whatever they thought you needed in the house that you had lost.

100
00:05:50.828 --> 00:05:51.966
The small items.

101
00:05:51.966 --> 00:05:54.241
Small appliances and things like that.

102
00:05:54.241 --> 00:05:55.759
Towels, all of that.

103
00:05:55.759 --> 00:05:58.793
Then with Dawn everything was geared toward her.

104
00:05:58.793 --> 00:06:02.207
Her toys and all of the things like that.

105
00:06:02.207 --> 00:06:03.345
Yeah very awesome.

106
00:06:03.345 --> 00:06:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Don't see that too much anymore.

107
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.319
<v LEE>No, you don't.

108
00:06:07.319 --> 00:06:08.198
You don't.

109
00:06:08.198 --> 00:06:11.716
There were several awards Richard received at Grambling.

110
00:06:11.716 --> 00:06:17.432
We already spoke about the awards he received at Mossville as a pirate.

111
00:06:17.432 --> 00:06:24.027
We talked about him throwing the shotput in single A when the school was small.

112
00:06:24.027 --> 00:06:36.339
It became larger in his junior and senior year when it was double A and he threw the shotput and all three times he won the state championship.

113
00:06:36.339 --> 00:06:43.374
The thing that was amazing about it is that as a senior with a broken leg,

114
00:06:43.374 --> 00:06:50.848
because he'd gotten his leg broken during football season, he was still able to win the shotput.

115
00:06:50.848 --> 00:06:57.444
That's amazing because you need that leg strength to be able to throw the shotput.

116
00:06:57.444 --> 00:07:05.358
But aside from that, I just listed some things that he had achieved as a player at Grambling.

117
00:07:05.358 --> 00:07:08.436
He won the Best Dressed Athlete award.

118
00:07:08.436 --> 00:07:10.195
Can you believe that?

119
00:07:10.195 --> 00:07:11.514
But he did.

120
00:07:11.514 --> 00:07:18.109
I shouldn’t say “believe it” because it was already meticulous about the way he dressed.

121
00:07:18.109 --> 00:07:23.825
He was a member of the Eastman Kodak 1960 [1968] All-American football team.

122
00:07:23.825 --> 00:07:31.739
This was for doing the college division for his athletic excellence, his self-discipline, his sportsmanship, and team loyalty.

123
00:07:31.739 --> 00:07:35.696
In 1968 in Atlanta [Georgia], he won the W.A.

124
00:07:35.696 --> 00:07:40.093
Scott II Memorial Trophy as one of the three captains.

125
00:07:40.093 --> 00:07:41.852
Henry Davis who was

126
00:07:41.852 --> 00:07:45.809
<c.p>drafted by the Giants was one of those captains.

127
00:07:45.809 --> 00:07:54.603
Ernest Sterling who was drafted by the Dallas Cowboys, a second captain and Richard Lee drafted by the Boston Patriots.

128
00:07:54.603 --> 00:07:59.000
Together those three captains at Grambling won that award.

129
00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:11.352
Richard was a member of the Acadiana Beagle Club where his fifteen inch male beagles were entered into several field trials in the '80s and '90s and they won first place,

130
00:08:11.352 --> 00:08:19.320
second, sometimes third and fourth at Rose City, [Greater Amite], Great Southern and the A.K.C [American Kennel Club] field trials.

131
00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.906
He was an N. R.A. [National Rifle Association] member.

132
00:08:22.906 --> 00:08:30.875
He was a member of the Huttig Hunting Club that's H-U-T-T-I-G Huttig Hunting Club in Arkansas where they hunted deer.

133
00:08:30.875 --> 00:08:36.055
He became Grambling's only three-time captain under the legendary coach Eddie Robinson's tenure.

134
00:08:36.055 --> 00:08:37.250
During his tenure.

135
00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:43.625
Coach Rob was there fifty-seven years, so within those fifty-seven years, and we've checked the record,

136
00:08:43.625 --> 00:08:48.406
that he was the only one that was a captain three times.

137
00:08:48.406 --> 00:08:59.164
When we look at the pen [ballpoint pen with inscription] sometimes we'll put the captain . . . not captain Rob but Coach Rob’s three-time captain [Richard Lee, Three-Time Captain].

138
00:08:59.164 --> 00:09:01.156
We’ll put it on that.

139
00:09:01.156 --> 00:09:06.734
Of course we know he raised beagles and I'm sorry the black mouth curs.

140
00:09:06.734 --> 00:09:12.711
Along with his cousin Bubba Jourdan who lived in Connecticut, [Richard] raised the Brittany spaniels.

141
00:09:12.711 --> 00:09:22.273
Now known to some as Hoss, H-O-S-S, some as Big Lee, some as Big Rich, some as Tender Lee, some as the Gentle Giant,

142
00:09:22.273 --> 00:09:26.258
some as Captain Lee; his granddaughters, Kiara and Monique Patterson,

143
00:09:26.258 --> 00:09:33.430
always called him Paw Paw Junie and [it] was Kiara who gave him that name Paw Paw Junie.

144
00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:37.016
It is escribed on his tombstone, Paw Paw Junie.

145
00:09:37.016 --> 00:09:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did Junie, is that just what she called him?

146
00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:46.343
<v LEE>Yeah and the thing about it I would say, "What do you want Richard Junior?”

147
00:09:46.343 --> 00:09:50.974
<c.p>And she just went Paw Paw Junie. [chuckles] Yeah and so that's it.

148
00:09:50.974 --> 00:09:54.892
Now Richard, I did mention earlier that Richard passed in December.

149
00:09:54.892 --> 00:09:56.673
On December the twelfth, 2010.

150
00:09:56.673 --> 00:10:01.303
After the interview I began to think about what happened prior to that.

151
00:10:01.303 --> 00:10:06.646
The month of October, Richard and I had been talking about buying a funeral plot.

152
00:10:06.646 --> 00:10:07.715
A burial plot.

153
00:10:07.715 --> 00:10:11.989
Paw Paw Lee, his mother's second husband that's in the picture there,

154
00:10:11.989 --> 00:10:19.113
he took care . . . helped her take care of the graveyard in Westlake [Louisiana] and would tell us all the time,

155
00:10:19.113 --> 00:10:21.607
"Richard you need to buy your plot.

156
00:10:21.607 --> 00:10:23.744
You need to buy your plot."

157
00:10:23.744 --> 00:10:33.718
It was just that year we had taken care of her because she died in 2006 and Pop died in 2009 so it was in 2010 we said,

158
00:10:33.718 --> 00:10:35.499
"We need to do this."

159
00:10:35.499 --> 00:10:42.266
So in October of 2010 we were in the graveyard and we said, "We need to get our plots."

160
00:10:42.266 --> 00:10:46.897
So Richard called Mr. Parsons who has now passed and bought our plot.

161
00:10:46.897 --> 00:10:54.377
Now that was October of 2010 and in October I had a dream and in this dream it was vision like.

162
00:10:54.377 --> 00:10:59.720
I was just sleeping and I'm in this big room maybe thirty by forty maybe.

163
00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:04.351
Just women and the women were in white or off-white or beige colors.

164
00:11:04.351 --> 00:11:06.488
Nobody in bright colors at all.

165
00:11:06.488 --> 00:11:12.900
I'm in this room and I'm all the way to the back and saying, "Why am I here?"

166
00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:17.530
All these women are here and I'm saying, "What is this all about?"

167
00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:22.517
And I looked up and all the way to the back Richard was standing;

168
00:11:22.517 --> 00:11:29.285
he was smaller as when I met him and he was in black with a white shirt and vest.

169
00:11:29.285 --> 00:11:31.422
White shirt black vest black pants.

170
00:11:31.422 --> 00:11:36.765
He was standing there and he was holding a baby and I was saying, "Ohh."

171
00:11:36.765 --> 00:11:41.040
Then all of a sudden it just felt like, "I'm a widow."

172
00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:53.863
I woke up shaking my head just like that and I never did mention that and I guess I pushed it aside and it wasn't until the day of his death that it just came back.

173
00:11:53.863 --> 00:11:56.000
I'm saying, "This was it."

174
00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.707
But who was this baby he was holding?

175
00:11:58.707 --> 00:12:05.814
So I talked to my daughter because he passed in Broussard at her home and I said, "Well Dawn what happened?

176
00:12:05.814 --> 00:12:07.844
I mean who was this baby?"

177
00:12:07.844 --> 00:12:11.567
I said did you have a child and I didn't know?

178
00:12:11.567 --> 00:12:12.920
She said, "No Momma."

179
00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:19.350
She said, "But you remember Uncle Kenny, Richard's brother, and Aunt Helen had a child and the baby died

180
00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:20.027
<c.p>within days.

181
00:12:20.027 --> 00:12:21.719
I said, "Oh my God.

182
00:12:21.719 --> 00:12:23.749
So he was holding his nephew."

183
00:12:23.749 --> 00:12:32.548
When we got back that Monday and Helen and Kenny and the family came over to meet us on Prater Road when we returned from Broussard.

184
00:12:32.548 --> 00:12:36.947
Everyone had gone but just Kenny and his wife and the immediate family.

185
00:12:36.947 --> 00:12:38.300
Those persons were there.

186
00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:41.346
I mentioned it to Helen and the tears flowed.

187
00:12:41.346 --> 00:12:45.745
I guess a mother who loses her child wonder is the baby okay?

188
00:12:45.745 --> 00:12:50.144
It was like God was revealing to me to tell her, "He's okay.

189
00:12:50.144 --> 00:12:51.498
He's with his uncle."

190
00:12:51.498 --> 00:12:52.852
To me that's amazing.

191
00:12:52.852 --> 00:12:53.190
Amazing.

192
00:12:53.190 --> 00:13:03.342
I found a note that he wrote and he put it in his folder with the G-men gathering was either he wrote it in 2009 or 2010 because he died.

193
00:13:03.342 --> 00:13:07.741
The folder says 2009, but he had it for 2010 and it says,

194
00:13:07.741 --> 00:13:15.186
these were his words he wrote and I don't know if he got it from someone or whatever but he wrote this.

195
00:13:15.186 --> 00:13:19.924
He said, "The deceased lives on in the minds and memories of the living.

196
00:13:19.924 --> 00:13:21.616
Physical death frees the soul."

197
00:13:21.616 --> 00:13:22.970
I was saying wow.

198
00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:25.000
It was something to see.

199
00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:31.741
Now at his funeral at Mount Calvary where we were members, the G-men were there.

200
00:13:31.741 --> 00:13:34.887
Many of them had played professional ball.

201
00:13:34.887 --> 00:13:42.976
They were all there in numbers and they came in with the family and then the Mossville Pirates.

202
00:13:42.976 --> 00:13:45.224
The Mossville Pirates were there.

203
00:13:45.224 --> 00:13:45.673
Tall.

204
00:13:45.673 --> 00:13:49.718
Standing tall in their jackets and they walked in.

205
00:13:49.718 --> 00:14:02.301
It was at his funeral on December eighteenth of 2010 that they changed the name from the G-Men Gathering to the Richard H. Lee Junior G- Men Gathering.

206
00:14:02.301 --> 00:14:07.245
This June will be our tenth year having the G-Men gathering.

207
00:14:07.245 --> 00:14:12.188
That's . . . I think I covered that as much as I could.

208
00:14:12.188 --> 00:14:19.379
I thought that would be important to even mention how life is and how things go.

209
00:14:19.379 --> 00:14:20.278
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah definitely.

210
00:14:20.278 --> 00:14:27.019
<v LEE>Just . . . Now speaking of Richard I thought about this and he often talked about it.

211
00:14:27.019 --> 00:14:29.715
<c.p>About his encounter with Howard Cosell.

212
00:14:29.715 --> 00:14:38.254
Muhammed Ali, I would always say, really has nothing on Richard in reference to his confrontations with Howard Cosell.

213
00:14:38.254 --> 00:14:47.692
In September of 1968 in New York at Yankee Stadium at the Whitney Young Classic where Grambling College played Morgan State,

214
00:14:47.692 --> 00:15:02.522
Cosell felt the staunch need to enter the locker room of the Grambling College Tigers after a controversial last minute call by officials that gave the win to Morgan State nine to seven;

215
00:15:02.522 --> 00:15:05.668
so you know those tigers weren't happy.

216
00:15:05.668 --> 00:15:13.758
Howard Cosell, with mic in hand, told the tigers, "Winning isn't important; it’s how you play the game."

217
00:15:13.758 --> 00:15:22.746
Well, Captain Lee, a Mossville native and around six [feet] five [inches] two hundred and ninety pound Grambling tackle countered.

218
00:15:22.746 --> 00:15:28.588
He said, "You need to get the H-hockey stick [hell] out of here.

219
00:15:28.588 --> 00:15:32.633
If winning isn't important, why do they keep score?

220
00:15:32.633 --> 00:15:35.329
Have you ever played a down?"

221
00:15:35.329 --> 00:15:38.475
With little to say, Cosell quickly exited.

222
00:15:38.475 --> 00:15:47.464
Note, the film later showed the discrepancy was that Grambling actually had won, but they could not reverse the call.

223
00:15:47.464 --> 00:15:55.104
The game became a part of a CBS Sports documentary called 1st and Goal in the Bronx.

224
00:15:55.104 --> 00:16:02.744
Over the years, Grambling College played Morgan State a total of fourteen games, winning thirteen straight games.

225
00:16:02.744 --> 00:16:06.788
Some would say they never lost to Morgan State.

226
00:16:06.788 --> 00:16:14.878
It was obvious and he was just . . . The thing about this is that the Grambling, not the Grambling,

227
00:16:14.878 --> 00:16:24.315
but the . . . Eddie Robinson museum has a copy of this and we were able to get a copy of that game;

228
00:16:24.315 --> 00:16:30.607
and so, Dawn which is awesome she was able to see her dad play.

229
00:16:30.607 --> 00:16:31.955
That was awesome.

230
00:16:31.955 --> 00:16:34.652
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he ever talk about that?

231
00:16:34.652 --> 00:16:36.000
That game?

232
00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:36.594
<v LEE>Oh yes.

233
00:16:36.594 --> 00:16:41.641
See that's the one I had flown in from Wisconsin and met them there in New York.

234
00:16:41.641 --> 00:16:41.938
Wow.

235
00:16:41.938 --> 00:16:43.719
It was obvious they had won.

236
00:16:43.719 --> 00:16:44.016
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

237
00:16:44.016 --> 00:16:45.500
<v LEE>It was just like wow.

238
00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:46.391
He was irate.

239
00:16:46.391 --> 00:16:48.469
It was just like he had just . . .

240
00:16:48.469 --> 00:16:49.656
<v ARSENEAULT>It's like an injustice.

241
00:16:49.656 --> 00:16:51.438
<v LEE>He could not contain his anger.

242
00:16:51.438 --> 00:16:52.328
He could not.

243
00:16:52.328 --> 00:16:53.516
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the call?

244
00:16:53.516 --> 00:16:55.000
Was it like a . . .

245
00:16:55.000 --> 00:17:00.174
<v LEE>It was . . . They were going in and it was . . . I think it was tied seven to seven.

246
00:17:00.174 --> 00:17:02.304
I'm thinking, or no it was not.

247
00:17:02.304 --> 00:17:03.217
What was it?

248
00:17:03.217 --> 00:17:04.435
I'm trying to think.

249
00:17:04.435 --> 00:17:11.130
No, it was nine to seven and so they were there and they could have kicked a . . . not kicked a field goal.

250
00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:15.391
They could have kicked a field goal, but they went in for a touchdown.

251
00:17:15.391 --> 00:17:19.348
They kept calling, “It wasn’t a first down, it wasn't a first down.”

252
00:17:19.348 --> 00:17:20.261
Actually it was.

253
00:17:20.261 --> 00:17:21.783
He had crossed the line.

254
00:17:21.783 --> 00:17:22.391
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

255
00:17:22.391 --> 00:17:24.522
<v LEE>Okay so they . . . That's how it is.

256
00:17:24.522 --> 00:17:31.826
Kicked the field goal, they would have won by one, but they felt that they could because they were such a good offensive line.

257
00:17:31.826 --> 00:17:34.261
<v ARSENEAULT>They're like, "Let's just go for the touchdown."

258
00:17:34.261 --> 00:17:34.565
<v LEE>Yeah.

259
00:17:34.565 --> 00:17:42.174
Go for this touchdown and it was so obvious and Rich said at the end of the game the officials came together in a huddle.

260
00:17:42.174 --> 00:17:46.130
They talked and they just ran off the field after they called it.

261
00:17:46.130 --> 00:17:50.391
<v ARSENEAULT>They knew they didn't want to be in the way of all these big . . .

262
00:17:50.391 --> 00:17:51.000
<v LEE>Yes!

263
00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:51.346
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

264
00:17:51.346 --> 00:17:52.383
<v LEE>Helmets were flying.

265
00:17:52.383 --> 00:17:55.149
It was like whoa and you saw that.

266
00:17:55.149 --> 00:17:57.569
We could see that on the field.

267
00:17:57.569 --> 00:17:59.298
We could see it’s like . . .

268
00:17:59.298 --> 00:18:03.101
<v ARSENEAULT>I can't imagine that must have been like something to see.

269
00:18:03.101 --> 00:18:04.138
<v LEE>It was something.

270
00:18:04.138 --> 00:18:12.436
During his tenure at Grambling, there was a time he talked about this often, he and Ernest Sterling who played for the Dallas Cowboys.

271
00:18:12.436 --> 00:18:16.239
When they were there at Grambling they substituted themselves as tackles.

272
00:18:16.239 --> 00:18:18.660
Two tackles, defensive tackles were in there.

273
00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:29.378
One I think his name was Clifford Gaspard from New Orleans [Louisiana] and another one from New Orleans that were being pounded by the opposing team and so Richard and Ernest,

274
00:18:29.378 --> 00:18:32.144
who were two of the captains, substituted themselves.

275
00:18:32.144 --> 00:18:39.059
Went in the game and told the refs, I don't know if they said, “Coach told you to get out,”

276
00:18:39.059 --> 00:18:47.356
or whatever but anyway they substituted themselves and it worked and Coach Rob admitted in all of his coaching career he had never had

277
00:18:47.356 --> 00:18:49.085
<c.p>a player to substitute himself.

278
00:18:49.085 --> 00:18:52.888
Then he told his captains, "It's a good thing it worked."

279
00:18:52.888 --> 00:18:54.617
They were . . . I tell you.

280
00:18:54.617 --> 00:18:56.000
They were something.

281
00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:56.786
Coach Rob.

282
00:18:56.786 --> 00:19:01.893
Some of Coach Rob's words were in addition to what we've already mentioned.

283
00:19:01.893 --> 00:19:10.929
The one thing he said and I think I have six listed here number one, "the greatest of all values is human courage."

284
00:19:10.929 --> 00:19:14.857
Number two, "Hey baby tell them something good about Grambling."

285
00:19:14.857 --> 00:19:21.536
Number three, "The will to win, the desire to succeed, the urge to reach your full potential.

286
00:19:21.536 --> 00:19:26.250
These are the keys that will unlock the door to personal excellence.”

287
00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:35.679
Number four, when someone said to Coach Rob that Joe Louis had a difficult time speaking, Coach responded, and he loved some Joe Louis.

288
00:19:35.679 --> 00:19:36.857
He loved him.

289
00:19:36.857 --> 00:19:39.607
He said, "Boxing is a violent sport.

290
00:19:39.607 --> 00:19:47.857
I would rather he say, ‘I wins’ than ‘I'm deeply saddened to inform you that I was overcome by my adversary.’"

291
00:19:47.857 --> 00:19:49.821
We did like, "Okay Coach."

292
00:19:49.821 --> 00:19:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Good point.

293
00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:57.043
<v LEE>Yes and number five, "You get out of life what you put into it."

294
00:19:57.043 --> 00:20:06.970
Number six, and I was at this game, they said at the press conference after his four hundredth win he said to everyone,

295
00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:12.149
"This is the greatest achievement I've experienced; and as I enjoy it,

296
00:20:12.149 --> 00:20:19.486
every player and every coach who has come through these doors should feel a part of this."

297
00:20:19.486 --> 00:20:28.982
He spoke this on Saturday, October the seventh, 1995, when we played against Mississippi Valley; and we beat them forty-two to six.

298
00:20:28.982 --> 00:20:32.435
The President, it was President Clinton, called him.

299
00:20:32.435 --> 00:20:34.161
We were all there.

300
00:20:34.161 --> 00:20:39.772
It was just an awesome place to be at Grambling on that day.

301
00:20:39.772 --> 00:20:40.204
Okay.

302
00:20:40.204 --> 00:20:50.994
Now some of his actions that Coach Rob did . . . some of the things he achieved [required] . . . the players had to dress up for away games.

303
00:20:50.994 --> 00:20:54.015
They had to wear a sports jacket.

304
00:20:54.015 --> 00:20:58.331
Rich would say, “If we lost Coach would say, ‘I

305
00:20:58.331 --> 00:21:00.489
<c.p>just didn't do my homework.’

306
00:21:00.489 --> 00:21:04.805
He would always say, ‘I just didn't do my homework.’”

307
00:21:04.805 --> 00:21:10.416
Sometimes they had two practices a day and then they had film viewing.

308
00:21:10.416 --> 00:21:16.459
Coach coached over four thousand players with over eighty percent receiving a college degree.

309
00:21:16.459 --> 00:21:19.912
Another thing I wrote down, just in 1967,

310
00:21:19.912 --> 00:21:29.839
that's one of the years that Rich was there because he was there from '64 to '68, thirty-two ex-Grambling players signed pro contracts.

311
00:21:29.839 --> 00:21:32.429
That was just in one year.

312
00:21:32.429 --> 00:21:39.766
He sent over two-hundred to the NFL, four NFL hall of famers, and one Super Bowl MVP.

313
00:21:39.766 --> 00:21:45.377
His second team in Grambling, remember he coached there for fifty-seven years, okay?

314
00:21:45.377 --> 00:21:50.988
His second team at Grambling became the Miracle Team because they were undefeated.

315
00:21:50.988 --> 00:21:54.441
Not just undefeated but untied and unscored on.

316
00:21:54.441 --> 00:22:00.915
One of the members of the Miracle Team was a part of the G-Men gathering.

317
00:22:00.915 --> 00:22:03.073
He died a year ago.

318
00:22:03.073 --> 00:22:06.957
His name is Adolph Byrd from Baton Rouge [Louisiana].

319
00:22:06.957 --> 00:22:11.274
That was something to see and to talk to him.

320
00:22:11.274 --> 00:22:13.000
It was awesome.

321
00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:19.637
At the end of his fifty-seven [year] coaching career at Grambling Coach Rob had four hundred eight wins,

322
00:22:19.637 --> 00:22:23.693
nine national black championships, and seventeen SWAC [Southwestern Athletic Conference] championships.

323
00:22:23.693 --> 00:22:32.542
He became the first college coach to take a team outside the continental United States to play, and they played in Japan in 1976.

324
00:22:32.542 --> 00:22:36.966
Richard had already gone, so I don't know what team they played.

325
00:22:36.966 --> 00:22:41.391
He had the ability to coax the best from his young players.

326
00:22:41.391 --> 00:22:45.816
He was considered the man with the golden mind, vision, and determination.

327
00:22:45.816 --> 00:22:51.715
It was just awesome, and I can see . . . seeing all of this and being at Grambling,

328
00:22:51.715 --> 00:22:55.034
why Johnny Comeaux in 1960 . . . When was it? '64.

329
00:22:55.034 --> 00:23:01.670
Sixty-three, sixty-four, wanted Rich not to go to Southern [University] but to make sure to go to Grambling.

330
00:23:01.670 --> 00:23:09.782
Now this is a coach who loved his players, the full player, and made sure that they got their work and graduated.

331
00:23:09.782 --> 00:23:10.888
That was awesome.

332
00:23:10.888 --> 00:23:13.469
Now getting back to a Mossville product.

333
00:23:13.469 --> 00:23:16.788
I don't know if you've heard of Garfield Verdine?

334
00:23:16.788 --> 00:23:19.000
Anyone told you about him?

335
00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:20.197
<v ARSENEAULT>I think so.

336
00:23:20.197 --> 00:23:20.596
<v LEE>Okay.

337
00:23:20.596 --> 00:23:22.192
<v ARSENEAULT>He was a musician?

338
00:23:22.192 --> 00:23:22.990
<v LEE>Yes, yes.

339
00:23:22.990 --> 00:23:26.981
Okay because he was a very close friend of Richard.

340
00:23:26.981 --> 00:23:29.375
A very close friend of mine.

341
00:23:29.375 --> 00:23:31.769
I have some things of his.

342
00:23:31.769 --> 00:23:32.966
I don't know.

343
00:23:32.966 --> 00:23:35.361
I'm just going to read that.

344
00:23:35.361 --> 00:23:36.558
<v ARSENEAULT>Sure, that's fine.

345
00:23:36.558 --> 00:23:36.957
<v LEE>Okay.

346
00:23:36.957 --> 00:23:42.144
Garfield was born October sixth, 1945 and he passed on November twenty-ninth, 1995.

347
00:23:42.144 --> 00:23:46.135
So he was born in 1945 and died in 1995.

348
00:23:46.135 --> 00:23:55.312
His musical talent and potential were discovered at age four, and I've got to talk to you about that in a few minutes,

349
00:23:55.312 --> 00:24:07.284
and by the tender age of seven he had begun to have a wide range of professional experiences as he played in churches and on local radio and television programs.

350
00:24:07.284 --> 00:24:22.447
His passion for music continued throughout his elementary and high school years and his musical ability flourished with exposure to the great sounds of music that came from nearby New Orleans and throughout the rest of the south.

351
00:24:22.447 --> 00:24:24.043
As an underage youth,

352
00:24:24.043 --> 00:24:39.606
he would stand outside of nightclubs listening to the music of many of the great blues singers and musicians as they bared their soul carrying their art form through the south on what became known as the Chitlin Circuit.

353
00:24:39.606 --> 00:24:41.202
You heard about that?

354
00:24:41.202 --> 00:24:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>[agrees]

355
00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:42.286
<v LEE>Okay.

356
00:24:42.286 --> 00:24:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>From Mr. Kenny Lee himself.

357
00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:44.428
<v LEE>Alright.

358
00:24:44.428 --> 00:24:53.411
Although he rarely got more than a glimpse of meeting the star performers, he loved and respected them and their music,

359
00:24:53.411 --> 00:24:55.978
not knowing that later in life,

360
00:24:55.978 --> 00:25:04.961
he would have the opportunity to perform with many of them and share the music and the magic that they endeared.

361
00:25:04.961 --> 00:25:11.806
After studying music and education at Grambling University, really it was Grambling College at the time,

362
00:25:11.806 --> 00:25:20.361
and Akron University of Ohio Garfield’s musical career took a majestic turn as he was chosen to do a series,

363
00:25:20.361 --> 00:25:25.494
a worldwide concert tour as he accompanied persons such as Arthur Prysock.

364
00:25:25.494 --> 00:25:36.189
That situation lasted several years providing a vast range of musical experiences as well as exposure and introduction to many other famed artists and musicians.

365
00:25:36.189 --> 00:25:45.172
Appreciation, respect for his musical abilities broadened as he later worked as accompanist to other famous blues artist including Percy Mayfield,

366
00:25:45.172 --> 00:25:52.444
Ruth Brown, “Big Mama” Thornton, Eddie “Cleanhead” Vinson, “Big Joe” Turner, Sippie Wallace, and Clarence “Gatemouth” Brown.

367
00:25:52.444 --> 00:25:55.011
I know you've heard of Gatemouth.

368
00:25:55.011 --> 00:26:01.000
He also appeared on The Tonight Show with Johnny Carson in the 1970s.

369
00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:12.878
Upon arriving in Denver in the early '70s he established a friendship with a musical alliance with drummer and the percussionist Shelly Rhym and multi-talented saxophonist [and composer] Homer Brown.

370
00:26:12.878 --> 00:26:24.359
The trio pleased local jazz for several years until Garfield and Homer left for Los Angeles [California] where they worked together on several recording projects before returning to Colorado.

371
00:26:24.359 --> 00:26:35.445
Continuing to expand his musical career in the early '90s Garfield toured internationally with his own jazz trio beginning in Canada and then on to the Far East,

372
00:26:35.445 --> 00:26:40.592
eventually playing a one year engagement in Hong Kong before returning to the

373
00:26:40.592 --> 00:26:41.384
<c.p>United States.

374
00:26:41.384 --> 00:26:48.906
When asked about his love of music and his career experiences, he would say joyfully, "It's been great fun.

375
00:26:48.906 --> 00:26:52.865
I would not hesitate to do it all over again.

376
00:26:52.865 --> 00:26:58.408
It is really a blessing to be able to do what you enjoy most.

377
00:26:58.408 --> 00:27:02.763
It is a continuous learning experience and you are never bored.

378
00:27:02.763 --> 00:27:12.661
Being able to personally share your music with others all around the world gives you the feeling of being an ambassador for the art form.

379
00:27:12.661 --> 00:27:15.829
I will always cherish the memories and experiences."

380
00:27:15.829 --> 00:27:22.955
When asked if he ever tired from touring and performing he would say, "I'm . . ." And I like this.

381
00:27:22.955 --> 00:27:26.122
"I'm like a flower in a flower pot.

382
00:27:26.122 --> 00:27:28.102
You can take me anywhere."

383
00:27:28.102 --> 00:27:33.249
His family lived on Old Spanish Trail right across from the recreation center.

384
00:27:33.249 --> 00:27:35.624
The swimming [pool] part of it.

385
00:27:35.624 --> 00:27:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He grew up in Mossville?

386
00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:41.018
<v LEE>Yes, in a part that was called Batville.

387
00:27:41.018 --> 00:27:42.904
A lot in Mossville too.

388
00:27:42.904 --> 00:27:45.544
That's where he and Rich were close.

389
00:27:45.544 --> 00:27:48.184
Rich often referred to him as Gator.

390
00:27:48.184 --> 00:27:50.825
The two of them shared childhood experiences.

391
00:27:50.825 --> 00:27:55.728
Rich checked on his property during Garfield's absence when he would go off.

392
00:27:55.728 --> 00:27:59.123
He was with him the summer before he died.

393
00:27:59.123 --> 00:28:04.781
He even joked with him on our front porch just two hours before he passed.

394
00:28:04.781 --> 00:28:07.798
We know that he passed November twenty-ninth, 1995.

395
00:28:07.798 --> 00:28:12.325
My daughter was one of his music students for a brief time.

396
00:28:12.325 --> 00:28:14.588
I don't . . . I greatly admired Garfield.

397
00:28:14.588 --> 00:28:19.868
His easy-going nature, his modesty about his talent, and I just treasured our friendship.

398
00:28:19.868 --> 00:28:27.035
Garfield returned home about a year, maybe two maybe three, years before he passed and he began closing doors.

399
00:28:27.035 --> 00:28:29.298
He began visiting his old friends.

400
00:28:29.298 --> 00:28:34.579
He reunited with his . . . the church he was a member of as a child.

401
00:28:34.579 --> 00:28:41.368
He began gathering songs he had written and shared with us in the '80s such as "Hey World,

402
00:28:41.368 --> 00:28:44.763
Jesus is Coming: Come Along and Rendezvous With Me."

403
00:28:44.763 --> 00:28:51.175
He wrote this for Pastor Harvey Gilliam who was once the pastor of Willow Springs Baptist Church.

404
00:28:51.175 --> 00:28:52.684
He wrote, "You Got

405
00:28:52.684 --> 00:28:57.588
<c.p>to be Ready When He Comes" and "There's a Doctor in the House."

406
00:28:57.588 --> 00:28:59.096
It was just something.

407
00:28:59.096 --> 00:29:04.000
He continued closing those chapters as he recounted childhood stories to us.

408
00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:06.924
Especially one he shared with us on Thanksgiving Day.

409
00:29:06.924 --> 00:29:10.823
Now this is the thing, just a few days before he passed,

410
00:29:10.823 --> 00:29:16.672
he was at our home and we had the windows up and it was a day like today.

411
00:29:16.672 --> 00:29:17.972
It was not cold.

412
00:29:17.972 --> 00:29:27.719
The windows up and some were sitting on the porch and some were in the den and in the dining room area and he was on the piano just playing.

413
00:29:27.719 --> 00:29:29.994
Oh he played whatever request we gave.

414
00:29:29.994 --> 00:29:32.268
But I've got to say this first.

415
00:29:32.268 --> 00:29:36.817
Anyways, he told us a story about his mother and I'm trying to think.

416
00:29:36.817 --> 00:29:43.640
I think we were together all that day from twelve-thirty noon until eight o'clock that night he was there, just playing.

417
00:29:43.640 --> 00:29:47.539
He told us this story and we gave the story a title.

418
00:29:47.539 --> 00:29:50.139
We called it after we heard about it.

419
00:29:50.139 --> 00:29:53.063
We called it, "I Just Don't Feel Like It."

420
00:29:53.063 --> 00:29:58.912
Garfield reminded us that when he was very young he often followed his mom to various church services,

421
00:29:58.912 --> 00:30:08.009
and it was during one of those services that he was asked by one of the elders to play and he said he just didn't feel like it.

422
00:30:08.009 --> 00:30:10.934
His mom looked at him and begged for him.

423
00:30:10.934 --> 00:30:23.931
He said he went over there quickly because he knew she wasn't supporting what he had done and what he had said and she grabbed him with a firm grab and told him what she had to say to him.

424
00:30:23.931 --> 00:30:26.530
She said, "Oh you don't feel like playing?"

425
00:30:26.530 --> 00:30:28.479
He confessed to us that day,

426
00:30:28.479 --> 00:30:35.628
"I don't think I ever again said 'I don't feel like playing.'" We got our chance to really enjoy him that day.

427
00:30:35.628 --> 00:30:42.451
He took our requests and we never knew that that would be our last time enjoying his presence in our home,

428
00:30:42.451 --> 00:30:45.375
because the next couple of days he was gone.

429
00:30:45.375 --> 00:30:47.000
He closed those chapters.

430
00:30:47.000 --> 00:30:49.661
<v ARSENEAULT>Who discovered that he had musical ability?

431
00:30:49.661 --> 00:30:54.983
<v LEE>He said, he told us his mom bought the piano for his two sisters.

432
00:30:54.983 --> 00:30:56.884
They were older than he

433
00:30:56.884 --> 00:30:57.645
<c.p>was, right?

434
00:30:57.645 --> 00:31:03.727
They just didn't feel like playing and he would get on it and she heard him.

435
00:31:03.727 --> 00:31:05.248
It was a gift.

436
00:31:05.248 --> 00:31:06.769
It was a gift.

437
00:31:06.769 --> 00:31:09.810
I mean he could work on any instrument.

438
00:31:09.810 --> 00:31:10.950
Play any instrument.

439
00:31:10.950 --> 00:31:17.413
He would tell me all the time, "T. I can't sing but I can play the instrument.

440
00:31:17.413 --> 00:31:19.694
I can make this thing talk."

441
00:31:19.694 --> 00:31:23.496
It was just a beautiful relationship we had with him.

442
00:31:23.496 --> 00:31:24.256
Beautiful relationship.

443
00:31:24.256 --> 00:31:27.298
<v ARSENEAULT>Good, I'm glad that got on the record.

444
00:31:27.298 --> 00:31:32.240
<v LEE>[agrees] I'll make sure that I get a picture of him there playing.

445
00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

446
00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:34.129
<v LEE>He was awesome.

447
00:31:34.129 --> 00:31:35.635
Another product of Mossville.

448
00:31:35.635 --> 00:31:43.539
Civil rights and integration, just a few other little things I need to add to what I mentioned earlier to you.

449
00:31:43.539 --> 00:31:47.679
I came into Calcasieu Parish School System after the cross over.

450
00:31:47.679 --> 00:31:52.948
Now the cross over . . . the cross over is when many integration . . . the integration itself.

451
00:31:52.948 --> 00:31:54.830
The schools were being mixed.

452
00:31:54.830 --> 00:32:00.100
Many heads of departments in the black schools were sent to the white schools.

453
00:32:00.100 --> 00:32:01.982
The heads of the departments.

454
00:32:01.982 --> 00:32:09.509
The less experienced teachers were left at those black schools while several new white teachers were sent to these schools.

455
00:32:09.509 --> 00:32:17.790
I arrived at Sulphur High 1972 where I worked as a librarian under the awesome leadership of a lady, Mrs. Barbara Thompson.

456
00:32:17.790 --> 00:32:23.059
She's a graduate of Southern University and she had a masters in Library Science.

457
00:32:23.059 --> 00:32:41.125
The Southern Association of Accreditation was scheduled to arrive at Sulphur High School that year but the librarian who was there before I don't know if she needed another degree or what it was but she took a sabbatical so they desperately needed a certified librarian so hey,

458
00:32:41.125 --> 00:32:42.255
<c.p>there I was.

459
00:32:42.255 --> 00:32:47.148
I worked in the library one year and the classroom until I retired.

460
00:32:47.148 --> 00:32:51.664
My tour at Sulphur High School it became a very rewarding one.

461
00:32:51.664 --> 00:33:01.827
I made a lot of lifelong friends and I think I touched a lot of lives of many young people so that to me was very rewarding.

462
00:33:01.827 --> 00:33:08.601
But there were moments during integration and things were rough, but it smoothed out and we worked together.

463
00:33:08.601 --> 00:33:11.613
The unity is the key to it all.

464
00:33:11.613 --> 00:33:15.000
Working together and understanding and appreciating the differences.

465
00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:16.955
Now securing the home loan.

466
00:33:16.955 --> 00:33:21.257
Rich and I had a difficult time securing a home loan.

467
00:33:21.257 --> 00:33:22.821
Our credit was great.

468
00:33:22.821 --> 00:33:28.296
I think we had one bill; it was the TV we had bought, okay?

469
00:33:28.296 --> 00:33:30.642
And we both had good jobs.

470
00:33:30.642 --> 00:33:36.117
Yet when Richard applied at the Sulphur location, First Federal, we got the runaround.

471
00:33:36.117 --> 00:33:41.592
His supervisor at Conoco said, "Well Richard" questioned him, "Why haven’t you built yet?"

472
00:33:41.592 --> 00:33:43.547
Richard related what had happened,

473
00:33:43.547 --> 00:33:53.324
so he suggested to Richard he would got to the Lake Charles location of First Federal and Richard did and “bam” we got the loan.

474
00:33:53.324 --> 00:34:00.363
When Rich went to the location in Sulphur I don't know if it was to pay a note.

475
00:34:00.363 --> 00:34:01.927
I don't think so.

476
00:34:01.927 --> 00:34:11.313
It was for some reason he had gone there and the worker who had taken our application was no longer there and guess what?

477
00:34:11.313 --> 00:34:18.352
Surprisingly so, she had been replaced by a Mossville resident and the resident is a cousin of Richard.

478
00:34:18.352 --> 00:34:25.000
We began to realize that in some cases the color green speaks louder than skin color.

479
00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:33.481
Now in black colleges, Grambling and Southern dominated sending players to the NFL, especially in the '60s and the '70s.

480
00:34:33.481 --> 00:34:36.873
I told you earlier the cars were coming.

481
00:34:36.873 --> 00:34:44.930
Those that had been drafted would come back with those beautiful cars driving on campus saying, "wow" you know.

482
00:34:44.930 --> 00:34:50.443
Then through integration, many of them began to be courted by white schools.

483
00:34:50.443 --> 00:34:51.291
White universities.

484
00:34:51.291 --> 00:34:53.411
The domination did not last.

485
00:34:53.411 --> 00:34:53.835
Okay.

486
00:34:53.835 --> 00:34:58.500
I tried to think of anything else but I could not.

487
00:34:58.500 --> 00:35:05.709
Now as I mentioned earlier, Vera and I began making a list of persons who have passed

488
00:35:05.709 --> 00:35:08.253
<c.p>since the Sasol buyout project began.

489
00:35:08.253 --> 00:35:12.918
Some of them at their deaths were still living in Mossville.

490
00:35:12.918 --> 00:35:14.614
Some had already moved.

491
00:35:14.614 --> 00:35:16.734
I have a list here.

492
00:35:16.734 --> 00:35:22.247
I know it's not complete but they're almost thirty people on that list.

493
00:35:22.247 --> 00:35:28.184
Lillie Mae Adams, Lillie Mae Jasmine Alexander, Donna Bates, Leeland “Mootie” Braxton, Annabel Ceaser,

494
00:35:28.184 --> 00:35:40.057
now these two people Mr. Braxton and Ms. Ceaser lived across the street from each other and died the same day or within a day of each other.

495
00:35:40.057 --> 00:35:41.329
It's like wow.

496
00:35:41.329 --> 00:35:49.810
Loritha Eagins, Polly Eaglin, Reverend Arthur H. Etienne he was the pastor of Mount Zion Baptist Church. [Allerine] Patsy Guillory,

497
00:35:49.810 --> 00:35:52.778
Isaac Guillory, John Joseph he died recently.

498
00:35:52.778 --> 00:35:56.595
Alean Jourdan, Pearl LaCour, Mabel Leday, Henry “Pushum” Mayo.

499
00:35:56.595 --> 00:35:59.987
Now he lived across the street from me.

500
00:35:59.987 --> 00:36:01.684
He was Richard's cousin.

501
00:36:01.684 --> 00:36:05.500
When he moved I was missing him and said,

502
00:36:05.500 --> 00:36:14.829
"Where’s Pushum?" and so his sons came by about a week or two weeks later and said, "Pushum moved a week ago."

503
00:36:14.829 --> 00:36:17.373
I'm saying, “I didn't even know.”

504
00:36:17.373 --> 00:36:20.766
It was like . . . just gradually they were leaving.

505
00:36:20.766 --> 00:36:26.278
He was the person who always watched our home when we were away.

506
00:36:26.278 --> 00:36:30.943
If we came back he'd say, "Richard, somebody came at 9:34."

507
00:36:30.943 --> 00:36:39.000
He never said they came at nine it was some odd number, 8:57 or something that sounded believable.

508
00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:42.243
Stafford Miles is another one who has passed.

509
00:36:42.243 --> 00:36:45.081
Leward Montgomery and his brother Vallery Montgomery.

510
00:36:45.081 --> 00:36:47.919
Both have passed and Anola Drake Moss.

511
00:36:47.919 --> 00:36:54.405
Reesie Mouton, this is the one who sponsored some of the parades that come through Mossville.

512
00:36:54.405 --> 00:36:59.676
She was a member at Mount Zion and she owned the flower shop.

513
00:36:59.676 --> 00:37:04.946
Thomas Parsons, he's the one who took care of the grave in Westlake.

514
00:37:04.946 --> 00:37:13.054
Calvin Payne, Archie Pryor, Della Gasaway Smith, Jean Jourdan Smith, Bernadine Tanner, Reverend Virgil Towner, that was Elaine's first cousin.

515
00:37:13.054 --> 00:37:14.676
Their parents were siblings.

516
00:37:14.676 --> 00:37:17.108
Percy Trahan, one of Richard’s brothers.

517
00:37:17.108 --> 00:37:23.189
Nathaniel Vincent, Flo Williams, and there's a lady I think is Rita from Bel Air.

518
00:37:23.189 --> 00:37:27.649
I think she passed or I just don't know the name.

519
00:37:27.649 --> 00:37:30.892
A man passed recently, Saturday, a [Hubert] Riggs.

520
00:37:30.892 --> 00:37:34.946
I don't know how long he had lived in Mossville.

521
00:37:34.946 --> 00:37:35.351
His

522
00:37:35.351 --> 00:37:36.162
<c.p>family had.

523
00:37:36.162 --> 00:37:39.405
Okay. [Lee adds he had lived in Queensboro]

524
00:37:39.405 --> 00:37:42.243
<v ARSENEAULT>I didn't know Mr. Vallery had passed.

525
00:37:42.243 --> 00:37:43.459
<v LEE>Yes he passed.

526
00:37:43.459 --> 00:37:43.865
Yes.

527
00:37:43.865 --> 00:37:45.081
<v ARSENEAULT>Didn't know that.

528
00:37:45.081 --> 00:37:46.297
<v LEE>He certainly did.

529
00:37:46.297 --> 00:37:47.919
I'm trying to think.

530
00:37:47.919 --> 00:37:54.000
He passed at about a couple weeks after his younger brother Leward Montgomery passed.

531
00:37:54.000 --> 00:37:56.098
<v ARSENEAULT>I had no idea.

532
00:37:56.098 --> 00:37:57.671
<v LEE>Yeah he passed.

533
00:37:57.671 --> 00:37:58.196
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

534
00:37:58.196 --> 00:37:59.244
<v LEE>Certainly did.

535
00:37:59.244 --> 00:38:05.538
I can't remember the date but we're looking about thirty, thirty-one people.

536
00:38:05.538 --> 00:38:08.684
It was like . . . I don't know.

537
00:38:08.684 --> 00:38:16.551
I know as I mentioned to you earlier how I expressed my feelings on how,

538
00:38:16.551 --> 00:38:32.284
what the move itself did to me and affected me and I wrote an open letter and I posted it on Facebook and I just have it entitled "Dear Mossville."

539
00:38:32.284 --> 00:38:36.480
For me this move has been very hard.

540
00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:43.822
I married Rich in 1969; we moved to Mossville on October thirty-first, 1970, Halloween.

541
00:38:43.822 --> 00:38:46.969
We built our home in 1973.

542
00:38:46.969 --> 00:38:51.689
So from 1973 to 2016, Prater Road was home.

543
00:38:51.689 --> 00:38:53.787
Rich, a Mossville native,

544
00:38:53.787 --> 00:38:55.884
<c.p>passed on in 2010.

545
00:38:55.884 --> 00:39:01.129
Leaving Mossville has been like leaving him all over again.

546
00:39:01.129 --> 00:39:13.716
The move began in disbelief, then to tears, and then to a feeling of being pressured as I observed the dismantling of my community.

547
00:39:13.716 --> 00:39:24.729
My neighbors began to move – first, those to the right; then, those across the street; and last, those to the left.

548
00:39:24.729 --> 00:39:29.449
We are one of the last families to leave.

549
00:39:29.449 --> 00:39:36.267
Prater Road and Mossville will always be a part of who we are.

550
00:39:36.267 --> 00:39:38.364
My prayers for Mossville.

551
00:39:38.364 --> 00:39:46.231
My prayers for peace, joy, and security go out for all my friends who remain.

552
00:39:46.231 --> 00:39:52.000
May God continue to bless and keep you, dear Mossville.

553
00:39:52.000 --> 00:39:54.158
<v ARSENEAULT>Thank you for sharing that.

554
00:39:54.158 --> 00:39:57.178
I don't really have any other questions.

555
00:39:57.178 --> 00:40:00.630
Is there anything else you want to share?

556
00:40:00.630 --> 00:40:01.062
<v LEE>No.

557
00:40:01.062 --> 00:40:03.651
<v ARSENEAULT>Feel like you need to say?

558
00:40:03.651 --> 00:40:04.082
<v LEE>No.

559
00:40:04.082 --> 00:40:07.534
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anyone respond to that post on Facebook?

560
00:40:07.534 --> 00:40:08.397
<v LEE>They did.

561
00:40:08.397 --> 00:40:17.459
My daughter her telephone is much more updated than mine, and so they did and agreed and you know with that.

562
00:40:17.459 --> 00:40:19.185
<v ARSENEAULT>There's similar kind of . . .

563
00:40:19.185 --> 00:40:19.616
<v LEE>Yeah.

564
00:40:19.616 --> 00:40:20.911
<v ARSENEAULT>Sentiment there maybe.

565
00:40:20.911 --> 00:40:30.404
<v LEE>Yeah I just could not after looking at that documentary of Mossville and how it appeared that everyone was happy and satisfied.

566
00:40:30.404 --> 00:40:39.034
That was not the full and the complete story and I wanted to share my feelings and so I said,

567
00:40:39.034 --> 00:40:42.055
"What better way could I do this?"

568
00:40:42.055 --> 00:40:43.349
Dawn said, “Facebook.

569
00:40:43.349 --> 00:40:45.938
Everyone is going to read Facebook.”

570
00:40:45.938 --> 00:40:48.527
So I said, "This is it.

571
00:40:48.527 --> 00:40:48.959
Facebook."

572
00:40:48.959 --> 00:40:51.548
This is these . . . This is it.

573
00:40:51.548 --> 00:40:55.000
This is how I feel about it.

574
00:40:55.000 --> 00:40:56.478
<v ARSENEAULT>Thank you for sharing.

575
00:40:56.478 --> 00:40:56.848
<v LEE>Alright.

576
00:40:56.848 --> 00:40:59.065
<v ARSENEAULT>I guess that wraps this up.

577
00:40:59.065 --> 00:40:59.435
<v LEE>Okay.

578
00:40:59.435 --> 00:41:01.283
<v ARSENEAULT>Then we're all set and . . .

579
00:41:01.283 --> 00:41:01.652
<v LEE>Okay.

580
00:41:01.652 --> 00:41:05.717
<v ARSENEAULT>Thank you Ms. Theresa and last chance to say . . . You're good?

581
00:41:05.717 --> 00:41:06.826
<v LEE>This is it.

582
00:41:06.826 --> 00:41:07.935
<v ARSENEAULT>This is it.

583
00:41:07.935 --> 00:41:08.304
Alright.

584
00:41:08.304 --> 00:41:11.261
Excellent I think we got a good interview.

585
00:41:11.261 --> 00:41:12.000
<v LEE>Okay.

586
00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:12.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4674.

587
00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:12.200
End Session II.]
